  "summary": "Elongated clay whistles from Tula — stretched ladies in raspberry-and-yellow striped dresses.",
  "description": "Filimonovo toywomen pull local oily clay into improbable stretches: ladies with bell skirts and tiny heads, soldiers, deer with branch antlers, peacocks — every figure impossibly long-necked because the clay itself demands it. Fired to pale ivory and painted in aniline brightness — raspberry red, egg yolk yellow, emerald — with striped skirts and simple face ticks, the toys double as whistles. The village near the Oka river kept the craft alive through the Soviet revival; Filimonovo figurines now star in Russian folk-art collections.",
